 is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363210 1071 "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is an $n\\times m$  matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$\n- **III.** $R$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363210 1074 "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ they span $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363210 1074 "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “$Q$ is an $n\\times m$  matrix” is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\n- Statement “$\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$” is true. Indeed, since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ they span $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement “$R$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix” is false. $R$ must be an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363238 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363238 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=6$\n- **II.** $T$ is a bijection\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363238 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ is false. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ and by the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not invertible. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $T$ is not invertible, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363238 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ and by the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not invertible. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $T$ is not invertible, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363384 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363384 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363384 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$ is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-2-3 \\\\ -10+1+9 \\\\ -5-1+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” is false. Since statement $5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$ is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=5,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent” is true. Since statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$; “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363384 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-2-3 \\\\ -10+1+9 \\\\ -5-1+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=5,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363405 1071 "![](images/8f/8f955ee719a4e69f0d09c678f0a74023f137efd7b5ae36d49b98706156384173.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363405 1071 "![](images/8f/8f955ee719a4e69f0d09c678f0a74023f137efd7b5ae36d49b98706156384173.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363405 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{0}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363405 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{0}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363619 1071 "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363619 1071 "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n- **I.** ${a}_{n}=4{a}_{n-1}+7$\n- **II.** ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-3}$\n- **III.** ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}-2n$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363619 1074 "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $3.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363619 1074 "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $3.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${a}_{n}=4{a}_{n-1}+7$; ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}-2n$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363626 1071 "Which of the following recurrence relations are linear (with constant coefficients)?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363626 1071 "Which of the following recurrence relations are linear (with constant coefficients)?\n- **I.** ${a}_{n}=-n{a}_{n-1}+4{a}_{n-2}$\n- **II.** ${b}_{n}=5{b}_{n-1}-2n$\n- **III.** ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}^{2}-3{c}_{n-2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363626 1074 "A recurrence relation is **linear (with constant coefficients)** if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a **forcing function**, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms. \n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not linear. The coefficient of the term $-n{a}_{n-1}$ is not constant.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is linear. The term ${b}_{n-1}$ occurs only in the first power with constant coefficient.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not linear. The term ${c}_{n-1}^{2}$ is not in the first power.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363626 1074 "A recurrence relation is **linear (with constant coefficients)** if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a **forcing function**, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms. \n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not linear. The coefficient of the term $-n{a}_{n-1}$ is not constant.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is linear. The term ${b}_{n-1}$ occurs only in the first power with constant coefficient.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not linear. The term ${c}_{n-1}^{2}$ is not in the first power.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${b}_{n}=5{b}_{n-1}-2n$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363633 1071 "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186363633 1071 "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n- **I.** ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n-2}+n$\n- **II.** ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+5{b}_{n-2}$\n- **III.** ${c}_{n}=6{c}_{n-1}+1$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363633 1074 "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $1$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186363633 1074 "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-