rms of the relation.\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The term of the expression contains a preceding term, ${c}_{n-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${b}_{n}=5{b}_{n-1}-{b}_{n-2}$; ${c}_{n}=\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{n-1}$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186364040 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186364040 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n- **I.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186364040 1074 "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}$ is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186364040 1074 "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}≢0.$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186364047 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186364047 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n- **I.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **II.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **III.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186364047 1074 "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=