ect answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366643 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not diagonalizable” is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, hence, is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$” is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement “$A$ must have $4$ real (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues” is true. Indeed, a $4\\times 4$ matrix has $4$ (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues and the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$”; “$A$ must have $4$ real (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366650 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366650 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n- **II.** $A$ must have $36$ real eigenvalues\n- **III.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u$ is parallel to $v$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366650 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but there are not $36$ of them. An $n\\times n$ matrix has at most $n$ distinct eigenvalues.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. So if the two eigenvalues correspond to different eigenvalues, they cannot be parallel.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366650 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable” is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement “$A$ must have $36$ real eigenvalues” is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but there are not $36$ of them. An $n\\times n$ matrix has at most $n$ distinct eigenvalues.\n\n- Statement “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u$ is parallel to $v$” is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. So if the two eigenvalues correspond to different eigenvalues, they cannot be parallel.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366657 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ real symmetric matrix whose roots of the characteristic polynomial have multiplicity equal to $1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366657 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ real symmetric matrix whose roots of the characteristic polynomial have multiplicity equal to $1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u$ is parallel to $v$\n- **II.** $A$ must have $7$ real and distinct eigenvalues\n- **III.** $A$ is orthogonal" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366657 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. But two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue will always be parallel if the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real. Also, since the roots of the characteristic polynomial have (algebraic) multiplicity all equal to $1,$ the eigenvalues are all distinct.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric 