}_{2}$ plane?\n- **I.** $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$\n- **II.** $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$\n- **III.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366718 1074 "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is positive for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is zero.\n\n- The equation $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-positive for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is  positive.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366718 1074 "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is positive for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is zero.\n\n- The equation $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-positive for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is  positive.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366739 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366739 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- **II.** If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- **III.** If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366739 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366739 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366773 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366773 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366773 1074 "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ is true. Indeed, The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $T$ is surjective and so  $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$” is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$; $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366773 1074 "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $T$ is surjective and so  $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366780 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366780 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨2,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366780 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $⟨2,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ is false. The point $(2,1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=2x$ since $1\\ne 2\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$” is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366780 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $(2,1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=2x$ since $1\\ne 2\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366794 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366794 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(D)=1$\n- **II.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366794 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(28-30)-2(7-5)+3(6-4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2-4+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366794 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(28-30)-2(7-5)+3(6-4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2-4+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $det(D)=0$ are true." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186366945 1071 "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186366945 1071 "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186367338 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the equation $(1+2x){y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186367338 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the equation $(1+2x){y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186367338 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}={e}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{4}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}=2\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186367338 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}={e}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{4}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}=2\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}$; $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186367345 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/3}$ is a solution to the equation $6{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186367345 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/3}$ is a solution to the equation $6{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186367345 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}={e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{-1}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}=5\\cdot {e}^{x-2x/3}=5{e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the 