s the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x} & \\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(e,e).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(e,e)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186368784 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x} & \\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(e,e),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(e,e)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{e} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{e}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(e,e)>0$ is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(e,e),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(e,e)=det({f}^{″}(e,e))=\\frac{1}{e}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{e})-{0}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$ is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)=({f}^{″}(e,e))_{11}=\\frac{1}{e}>0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$” is true. Indeed, since $H(e,e)<0,$ the point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$; “The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186368784 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x} & \\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{