ch of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P({a,{a,b}})={∅,{a},{{a,b}},{a,{a,b}}}$\n- **II.** $P({a,{b}})={∅,{a},{{a,b}}}$\n- **III.** $P({{∅}})={∅,{{∅}}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372412 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $P({a,{a,b}})={∅,{a},{{a,b}},{a,{a,b}}}$ is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${a,{a,b}}$ is ${∅,{a},{{a,b}},{a,{a,b}}}.$\n\n- Statement $P({a,{b}})={∅,{a},{{a,b}}}$ is false. Notice that we are given the set ${a,{b}}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{a},{{b}},{a,{b}}}.$\n\n- Statement $P({{∅}})={∅,{{∅}}}$ is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${{∅}}$ is ${∅,{{∅}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P({a,{a,b}})={∅,{a},{{a,b}},{a,{a,b}}}$; $P({{∅}})={∅,{{∅}}}$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372412 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${a,{a,b}}$ is ${∅,{a},{{a,b}},{a,{a,b}}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${a,{b}}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{a},{{b}},{a,{b}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${{∅}}$ is ${∅,{{∅}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372419 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372419 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P(P(∅))={∅,{∅}}$\n- **II.** $P(P({∅}))={∅,{∅}}$\n- **III.** $P(P({0}))={∅,{∅},{{0}},{∅,{0}}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372419 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $P(P(∅))={∅,{∅}}$ is true. Indeed, the power set of the set $∅$ is ${∅}.$ Therefore, $P(P(∅))={∅,{∅}}.$\n\n- Statement $P(P({∅}))={∅,{∅}}$ is false. Notice that we are given the set ${∅}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{∅}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({∅}))={∅,{∅},{{∅}},{∅,{∅}}}.$\n\n- Statement $P(P({0}))={∅,{∅},{{0}},{∅,{0}}}$ is true. Notice that we are given the set ${0}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{0}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({0}))={∅,{∅},{{0}},{∅,{0}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(P(∅))={∅,{∅}}$; $P(P({0}))={∅,{∅},{{0}},{∅,{0}}}$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372419 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set $∅$ is ${∅}.$ Therefore, $P(P(∅))={∅,{∅}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${∅}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{∅}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({∅}))={∅,{∅},{{∅}},{∅,{∅}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set ${0}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{0}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({0}))={∅,{∅},{{0}},{∅,{0}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372426 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372426 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P(P(2))={∅,{2}}$\n- **II.** $P(P({2}))={∅,{∅},{{2}},{∅,{2}}}$\n- **III.** $P(P({{2}}))={∅,{{2}}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372426 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $P(P(2))={∅,{2}}$ is false. Notice that $2$ is not a set. So, the notation $P(P(2))$ does not make sense.\n\n- Statement $P(P({2}))={∅,{∅},{{2}},{∅,{2}}}$ is true. Notice that we are given the set ${2}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{2}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({2}))={∅,{∅},{{2}},{∅,{2}}}.$\n\n- Statement $P(P({{2}}))={∅,{{2}}}$ is false. Notice that we are given the set ${{2}}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{{2}}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({{2}}))={∅,{∅},{{{2}}},{∅,{{2}}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(P({2}))={∅,{∅},{{2}},{∅,{2}}}$ only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372426 1074 "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $2$ is not a set. So, the notation $P(P(2))$ does not make sense.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that we are given the set ${2}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{2}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({2}))={∅,{∅},{{2}},{∅,{2}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that we are given the set ${{2}}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{{2}}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({{2}}))={∅,{∅},{{{2}}},{∅,{{2}}}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372641 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =1,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372641 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =1,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The stability function is $R(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.$\n\n- **II.** The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\n\n- **III.** The method is absolutely stable.\n\n- **IV.** The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.50.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372641 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.50.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372641 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability function is $R(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.$” is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.50.$ Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.50.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.$”; “The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372663 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function for Euler's method is given by $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186372663 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda }$\n- **II.** $Δx=\\frac{0.6}{\\lambda }$\n- **III.** $Δx=\\frac{3}{\\lambda }$\n- **IV.** $Δx=\\frac{1.9}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function for Euler's method is given by $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186372663 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambd