 is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}0=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186373540 1074 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}0=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” only." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186373757 1071 "At which points on the curve $2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186373757 1071 "At which points on the curve $2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,1)$\n- **II.** $(0,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$\n- **IV.** $(1,1)$" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186374261 1071 "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\nI. $\\{3,4,5\\}$ and $\\{6,7,8\\}$\n\nII. $\\{-4,-3,-2\\}$ and $\\{2,3,4\\}$\n\nIII. $[-3,0]$ and $[0,3]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186374261 1071 "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186374261 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Pair I is disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{3,4,5\\}\\cap \\{6,7,8\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair II is also disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{-4,-3,-2\\}\\cap \\{2,3,4\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair III is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n[-3,0]\\cap [0,3]=\\{0\\}\\ne ∅\