17592186576411 false] [17592186376047 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186376047 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186376047 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 false] [17592186376047 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186376054 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,-1,2)$ and $(1,2,0).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 17592186576411 true] [17592186376054 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,-1,2)$ and $(1,2,0).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537074 175921865764